 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_kamma.48b.2",
   "daf": "48b",
   "sentenceId": 1,
   "he": "נָפַל לְבוֹר וְהִבְאִישׁ מֵימָיו – חַיָּיב. אָמַר רָבָא: לֹא שָׁנוּ אֶלָּא שֶׁהִבְאִישׁ בִּשְׁעַת נְפִילָה, אֲבָל לְאַחַר נְפִילָה – פָּטוּר. מַאי טַעְמָא? הָוֵי שׁוֹר ״בּוֹר״ וּמַיִם ״כֵּלִים״, וְלֹא מָצִינוּ בּוֹר שֶׁחִיֵּיב בּוֹ אֶת הַכֵּלִים.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: If the ox that he brought into the courtyard without permission «fell into» the owner’s «pit and contaminated its water,» the owner of the ox is «liable. Rava says: They taught» this halakha «only» in a case «where» the ox «contaminated» the water «at the time of the fall. But» if it contaminated the water «after the fall,» e.g., the animal died there and the decomposing carcass despoiled the water, he is «exempt. What is the reason?» The «ox,» in this case, «is» considered as a «pit, and the water» has the status of «utensils» that fall into a pit, «and we have not found» a case of damage categorized as «Pit that renders one liable» for causing damage to «utensils.» Therefore, he is exempt."
  }
 ],
